('a37d721b-3834-4086-b12d-cf25e2e146c0','image',$c$A 20 km/h mode isn't a speed limit. The iScooter i8M ($399.99, 12.55kg) has 10, 20 and 30 km/h modes, but the rider can switch between them and it can't be locked to 25 km/h. Victoria's rules are about what the scooter is capable of, so we list it as private property only. Check any model against your state at scootfinder.au. Did you know a mode doesn't count as a limit?$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/8d22722/posts/2026-10-12/scootfinder/p03-tue-am-i8m-20kmh-mode.jpg'],'2026-10-12T22:00:00+00','queued','roxy','sf-2026-10-13-am-facebook',$a$Roxy preflight 11 Oct: full size eye audit of every slide/frame, live feed dupe check (2 reels live), facts per manifest sources, white bg corners, no dashes. Hook: A 20 KM/H MODE ISN’T A LIMIT.$a$),
('5649f5ce-3f06-4d07-83dc-1e02e66ad18c','image',$c$A 20 km/h mode isn't a speed limit.

The iScooter i8M is $399.99, weighs 12.55kg and has 10, 20 and 30 km/h riding modes. Sounds like a scooter you could slot into the 25 km/h class. But the rider can change the mode, and the scooter can't be locked to 25.

Victoria's rule is about what the scooter is capable of: a device that can exceed 25 km/h is not a legal e-scooter on public paths. So ScootFinder lists it as private property only.

It is the cheapest full size scooter in our directory, and the most expensive lesson if you buy it for the commute. Did you know a software mode does not count as a limit?

